---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import SectionHeader from '../components/SectionHeader.astro';
---

<Base
  title="Method"
  description="How Noemium grades tools: ship / situational / skip, what counts as a receipt, and why a PR is the unit of truth."
>
  <SectionHeader
    title="Method"
    sub="The ledger, not the vibe. How a card earns a verdict, a receipt, and a place on the floor."
  />

  <article class="max-w-2xl space-y-10 pb-16 text-[15px] leading-relaxed">
    <section>
      <h2 class="font-mono text-[13px] font-bold tracking-[0.14em] text-ink uppercase">Verdicts</h2>
      <dl class="mt-4 space-y-4">
        <div>
          <dt class="font-display text-xl font-extrabold tracking-tight uppercase">Ship</dt>
          <dd class="mt-1 text-ink opacity-80">
            We run it and would run it again for the job on the card. Limitations are still
            printed — ship is not a trophy.
          </dd>
        </div>
        <div>
          <dt class="font-display text-xl font-extrabold tracking-tight uppercase">Situational</dt>
          <dd class="mt-1 text-ink opacity-80">
            It wins in a named setup and loses outside it. Read the limitations before you pay.
          </dd>
        </div>
        <div>
          <dt class="font-display text-xl font-extrabold tracking-tight uppercase">Skip</dt>
          <dd class="mt-1 text-ink opacity-80">
            We would pick something else for the same job. The card stays so you can see why —
            and who we point to instead, when we have a peer.
          </dd>
        </div>
      </dl>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2 class="font-mono text-[13px] font-bold tracking-[0.14em] text-ink uppercase">Receipts</h2>
      <p class="mt-4 text-ink opacity-80">
        A receipt is a URL that backs a printed fact. Pricing pages first. Vendor docs, licenses,
        and git repos count. A marketing homepage is a weak receipt: we still show it, we just
        don't let it pretend to be a price.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-3 text-ink opacity-80">
        If we can't show a source, we don't print the number. Click any VERIFIED stamp for the
        date, the observer, the footnotes, and the file on GitHub.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2 class="font-mono text-[13px] font-bold tracking-[0.14em] text-ink uppercase">
        Verification
      </h2>
      <p class="mt-4 text-ink opacity-80">
        Source-verified means receipts back every printed fact. Field-tested means we ran it
        hands-on. The <a href="/verified/" class="text-accent hover:underline">verified shelf</a>
        lists only field-tested cards; empty counts are shown honestly.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2 class="font-mono text-[13px] font-bold tracking-[0.14em] text-ink uppercase">
        No stars
      </h2>
      <p class="mt-4 text-ink opacity-80">
        A 4.3 from nine reviews is an invoice costume. We don't average vibes. The unit of truth
        is a pull request: one file, one observer, one date, named limitations. Wrong? Edit the
        page. The history is public.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2 class="font-mono text-[13px] font-bold tracking-[0.14em] text-ink uppercase">
        Paid placements
      </h2>
      <p class="mt-4 text-ink opacity-80">
        Zero. Anchor cards on the floor are an editorial mix so the shelf isn't only famous
        names — not a slot you can buy. Affiliate links are declared in the YAML and marked on
        the page. They never move a verdict. Every declined paid-influence offer is logged in the
        <a href="/refusals/" class="text-accent hover:underline">Open Refusal Ledger</a>.
      </p>
    </section>

    <p class="font-mono text-[13px] text-ink-dim">
      <a href="/about/" class="text-accent hover:underline">About</a>
      <span class="mx-2" aria-hidden="true">·</span>
      <a href="/contribute/" class="text-accent hover:underline">Contribute a card</a>
    </p>
  </article>
</Base>
